Title: THE PROPOSAL
Author: Anton Chekhov
Draft date: 1888
Contact: Public Domain
Notes: A Jest in One Act. Translated from the Russian.

===

FADE IN:

INT. CHUBUKOV'S COUNTRY HOUSE - DAY

A well-appointed drawing room. STEPAN STEPANOVITCH CHUBUKOV, a landowner, stands near a window. He is a stout, jovial man in his sixties. His neighbor, IVAN VASSILEVITCH LOMOV, enters wearing a dress coat and white gloves. He is nervous, excitable, and prone to palpitations.

CHUBUKOV
(going to meet him, beaming)
Ivan Vassilevitch! What a surprise! My dear fellow! This is a pleasure!

He shakes LOMOV's hand warmly.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
What brings you here? What a treat! Sit down, sit down, do!

LOMOV
Thank you. And how are you, Stepan Stepanovitch?

CHUBUKOV
Oh, bearing up, bearing up, my angel, thanks to your prayers and all the rest of it. Do sit down...

LOMOV
I must tell you, you know I've come to ask a favor of you. It's nothing very serious but—

He fidgets with his gloves and mops his brow.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
I am dreadfully excited, as you see... To cut the matter short, I have come to ask the hand of your daughter, Natalia Stepanovna.

CHUBUKOV
(joyfully)
My darling! Ivan Vassilevitch! Say it once more — I didn't quite hear you!

LOMOV
I have the honor to ask—

CHUBUKOV
(interrupting, embracing him)
My dearest, dearest man! How delightful! And so on, and so on, and all the rest of it! I have been hoping for it for a long time! It has been my constant desire!

He wipes a tear from his eye.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
I have always loved you as if you were my own son, my dear fellow! God bless you both! This is my heart's desire! Why am I standing here like a blockhead?

He calls toward the door.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
(loudly)
Natalia! Natalia, come here!

LOMOV
(alarmed)
But, Stepan Stepanovitch, what do you think? May I count on her consent?

CHUBUKOV
A fine fellow like you — and she won't consent! She's in love to the point of foolishness! And so on...

He exits to fetch NATALIA.

LOMOV
(alone, clutching his chest)
It's cold... I'm trembling all over, as if I had to take an examination. The great thing is to make up my mind. If I keep thinking about it, talking about it, waiting for the ideal or for true love — I shall never get married...

He paces.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
I am thirty-five years old. I need a quiet, settled life. My heart is weak — I have palpitations all the time; I am excitable and always getting upset terribly...

He sits down, then stands again.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
The right thing would be to get married now, while I still have my wits about me. Natalia Stepanovna is a good housekeeper, not bad-looking, well-educated...

He sighs.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
What more do I want?

He hears footsteps and straightens his coat.

NATALIA STEPANOVNA enters. She is twenty-five, robust, and practical. She carries embroidery.

NATALIA
Oh, it's you, Ivan Vassilevitch! Papa said there was a merchant come to collect some goods. Good morning, Ivan Vassilevitch!

LOMOV
Good morning, Natalia Stepanovna.

NATALIA
Excuse my apron and undress... we are shelling peas for drying. Why haven't you visited us for so long? Sit down.

They both sit.

NATALIA (CONT'D)
Will you have some lunch?

LOMOV
No, thank you. I have already had some lunch.

NATALIA
Then smoke... Here are the matches. The weather is splendid now, but yesterday it was so wet that the workmen didn't work at all. How many stacks have you cut? I got carried away and had the whole rye cut, and now I'm not at all sure I was right to do it. What do you think? Wasn't it too soon?

LOMOV
(anxiously)
Yes... yes, yes... My heart is beating dreadfully... Natalia Stepanovna, I must tell you that I have decided to ask you to hear me out... of course, you will be surprised, and perhaps even angry, but I...

He is trembling violently.

NATALIA
What is it?

LOMOV
I will try to be brief. As you know, I have long since had the honor of knowing your family, Natalia Stepanovna. My late aunt and her husband, from whom, as you know, I inherited my land, always had the greatest respect for your father and your late mother. The Lomovs and the Chubukovs have always maintained the most friendly, and I might almost say the most affectionate, relationship. And furthermore, as you know, my land adjoins your own. You may remember, my Oxen Meadows border upon your Birchwood.

NATALIA
Excuse me. You said "my Oxen Meadows"... Are they really yours?

LOMOV
Yes, they are mine.

NATALIA
Well, that's news to me. The Oxen Meadows are ours, not yours!

LOMOV
No, they are mine, dear Natalia Stepanovna.

NATALIA
Well, this is the first time I have heard that! Where did this come from?

LOMOV
Where? I mean the Oxen Meadows that are wedged in between your Birchwood and the Burnt Swamp.

NATALIA
Yes, yes, yes... They are ours.

LOMOV
No, you are mistaken, my dear Natalia Stepanovna — they are mine.

NATALIA
Just think, Ivan Vassilevitch! How long have they been yours?

LOMOV
How long? As long as I can remember they have always been ours.

NATALIA
Well, I must say, that really is surprising!

LOMOV
But you can see from the documents, Natalia Stepanovna. The Oxen Meadows were once the subject of dispute, that's true, but now everyone knows they are mine. There's nothing to dispute. You see, my aunt's grandmother, when she gave your father's grandfather's peasants the use of the Meadows for forty years free of charge, in return the peasants were to make some bricks for her. Now your grandfather's peasants used the Meadows for forty years and got into the habit of looking upon them as their own — and then when the Government survey was made—

NATALIA
It was quite wrong! My grandfather and my great-grandfather considered the land to the Burnt Swamp to be theirs — so the Meadows are ours! What is there to dispute about? I don't understand! It's really quite annoying!

LOMOV
I will show you the documents, Natalia Stepanovna!

NATALIA
No, you are simply joking, or trying to tease me! A nice way to treat us! The Meadows are ours, and that's all about it. I don't want your documents.

LOMOV
But I assure you—

NATALIA
But I assure you they are ours!

LOMOV
There is no need to shout, Natalia Stepanovna. We can discuss this matter calmly, without raising our voices. I simply wish to—

NATALIA
Calmly? You come into my house and start claiming our land is yours, and you expect me to be calm? I don't understand how you can be so cool about it. Our neighbor comes to claim our own Meadows and expects us to be calm!

LOMOV
The Meadows are mine! I will prove it!

NATALIA
They are ours! You can prove what you like! I don't care!

CHUBUKOV enters in response to the raised voices.

CHUBUKOV
What is going on here? Why are you shouting?

NATALIA
Papa, please explain to this gentleman: whose are the Oxen Meadows — ours or his?

CHUBUKOV
(astounded)
My darling, the Meadows are ours!

LOMOV
But, Stepan Stepanovitch, how can they be yours? Be reasonable! My aunt's grandmother gave the use of the Meadows to your grandfather's peasants—

CHUBUKOV
Allow me, my precious! You forget that your grandmother's peasants never paid anything because there was a dispute about the Meadows, and so on. And now every dog knows that they're ours! You evidently haven't looked at the map!

LOMOV
I will prove to you that they are mine!

CHUBUKOV
Don't shout, my dear man!

LOMOV
If you try to talk sense into me, I will shout ten times as loud!

CHUBUKOV
It is not my fault, my dear man! I didn't begin it! You did! So don't shout.

LOMOV
(clutching his heart)
Palpitations! My heart is going... Excuse me, I must sit down...

He sits.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
(more quietly)
They are mine. My documents prove it.

CHUBUKOV
They are ours! And I don't care a straw for your documents, my dear fellow!

LOMOV
Your grandfather's peasants baked bricks for my grandmother. My grandmother, wishing to please them—

CHUBUKOV
Your grandmother, your grandfather, your great-aunt — I don't care a hang for all your family! The Meadows are ours, and that's the end of it!

LOMOV
The Meadows are mine!

CHUBUKOV
Ours!

LOMOV
Mine!

CHUBUKOV
(loudly)
You shall not have them! I will have you up before the court! I'll put the whole matter before the Justice of the Peace!

LOMOV
(pressing his chest)
Before the Justice—! Before the Justice—! You may!

CHUBUKOV
Swindler!

LOMOV
(losing composure entirely)
Old rat! Landjobber! Old turkey!

CHUBUKOV
Rascal! Scarecrow! Monster!

NATALIA
(joining in)
Monster! Scarecrow!

LOMOV
(staggering)
My heart! My leg has gone to sleep! I can't walk! Water!

CHUBUKOV
Cheat!

NATALIA
Monster!

LOMOV collapses into a chair, gasping. His leg twitches. He tries to speak but cannot.

CHUBUKOV
(to NATALIA)
Good riddance!

He begins to pace furiously.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Squire and all! I don't want to see him again!

NATALIA
(also pacing)
Swindler!

A beat of silence.

CHUBUKOV
(stopping suddenly)
And yet... he came to make you a proposal.

NATALIA
(stopping)
A proposal? To whom?

CHUBUKOV
Why, to you, of course! He came to propose to you, and then you two started quarreling about the Meadows and all the rest of it. That's why he came — to make you a proposal!

NATALIA
(horrified)
To make me a proposal? Me?

CHUBUKOV
Of course! So you've been shouting at a suitor! What a fool! What an idiot!

NATALIA
(covering her face)
What have I done! What have I done! Bring him back! Bring him back! Oh!

She wails.

CHUBUKOV
(calling into the hallway)
Bring him back! Ivan Vassilevitch! Come back! Come back!

He rushes to the door.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
Ivan Vassilevitch! Come back, I tell you!

LOMOV reappears, pale and still clutching his chest.

LOMOV
These palpitations! These trembles! Where is my leg? My leg!

CHUBUKOV
(in a rush)
My dear man! We were all to blame... I'm sorry... I beg your pardon! My tongue ran away with me...

He extends his hand.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
The Meadows are yours! Yes, yes, they are yours! Sit down, please!

NATALIA
(quickly)
The Meadows are yours, Ivan Vassilevitch. Please sit down.

LOMOV sinks into a chair, still pressing his chest.

LOMOV
My heart... My leg... I see stars...

NATALIA
The Meadows are yours. Sit down.

She kneels beside him.

LOMOV
(weakly)
I only wanted to say that... that... I love you, Natalia Stepanovna. I wanted to make you a proposal...

NATALIA
(overcome)
I love you too, Ivan Vassilevitch! Say it again!

LOMOV
I... I have always respected you... respected your family... Your father is a fine man... and I...

He gasps and nearly faints.

CHUBUKOV
(clapping his hands)
He's dead! Good God! Water!

He rushes for water.

NATALIA
(shaking LOMOV's arm)
Ivan Vassilevitch! Ivan Vassilevitch! Say something! He's dead!

CHUBUKOV returns and throws water on LOMOV's face.

LOMOV
(spluttering)
Wh—What? Who? Where?

CHUBUKOV
(beaming)
Get married! Quick! She's willing! Give me your hand!

He takes LOMOV's hand and shoves it toward NATALIA's.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
She consents, and so on! I give you my blessing, and all the rest of it! Only for God's sake leave me in peace!

NATALIA
(taking LOMOV's hand)
Ivan Vassilevitch! I am happy!

LOMOV
(dazed)
What? Who? Oh... yes... I am very happy too...

He blinks and looks at NATALIA properly.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
My heart... but I am... very, very happy...

He takes her hand. A beat.

LOMOV (CONT'D)
You know, by the way... your dog Otkatai is lame.

NATALIA
(withdrawing her hand)
He is not lame! He is the finest dog we ever had!

LOMOV
He is lame, I tell you! At the last hunt he went lame in his left foreleg.

NATALIA
He is not lame!

CHUBUKOV
(throwing up his hands)
They're at it again! They're at it again!

NATALIA
(rising)
The best dog in the district, and he calls him lame!

LOMOV
Our Ugadi is better — without a doubt!

NATALIA
Ugadi is old and as ugly as a worn-out saddle!

LOMOV
(outraged)
Ugadi is old, but I would not swap five Otkatis for him!

NATALIA
(shrieking)
What!

CHUBUKOV
(shouting above them both)
What is this? Champagne! Champagne!

He pours three glasses hurriedly.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
Here! Drink this! And then shake hands! Husband and wife! You love each other!

He thrusts glasses at them both.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
You can argue about your dogs afterward! All your life if you like!

NATALIA and LOMOV take the glasses, glowering at each other. Then, slowly, they drink.

LOMOV
(lowering his glass)
Otkatai is still lame.

NATALIA
He is not.

CHUBUKOV
(to the heavens)
They're married!

He raises his own glass.

CHUBUKOV (CONT'D)
God help them!

He drinks.

FADE OUT.

